'''''Begonia'''''  is a [[genus]] of [[perennial]] [[flowering plant]]s in the family [[Begoniaceae]]. The genus contains more than 2,000 different plant species, distributed primarily across tropical and subtropical regions of Africa, Asia, and Central and South America, with Africa considered the center of origin.<ref name="Lingfei">{{cite book |editor1-last=Li |editor1-first=Lingfei |editor2-last=Yang |editor2-first=Leilei |editor3-last=Ruihua |editor3-first=Ding |title=An Illustrated Book of Begonias |date= December 2023 |publisher=China Agricultural Press |location=Beijing |isbn=978-7-109-31617-1 |pages=002–005 |edition=1 |ref=Lingfei}}</ref> Some species are commonly grown indoors as ornamental [[houseplants]] in cooler climates, or cultivated outside in summertime for their bright colorful flowers, which have [[petal]]s but no [[sepal]]s.
[[File:Begonia johnstonii Flower 01.jpg|left|thumb|A female flower on the left and a male flower on the right, from a ''[[Begonia johnstonii]]'' plant.]]
[[File:Petals_0016.jpg|thumb|right|Pink flowering Begonia]]

== Description ==
With 2,000+ species, ''Begonia'' is one of the [[List of the largest genera of flowering plants|largest genera of flowering plants]].{{sfnp|Frodin|2004}}<ref>{{cite web |url= http://padme.rbge.org.uk/begonia/ |title=Begonia | website= padme.rbge.org.uk| publisher= [[Royal Botanic Garden Edinburgh]] | date= | access-date= 2026-02-02}}</ref> The species are terrestrial (sometimes [[epiphytic]]) [[herb]]s or undershrubs, and occur in subtropical and tropical moist climates, in [[South America|South]] and [[Central America]], [[Africa]], and southern [[Asia]]. Terrestrial species in the wild are commonly upright-[[plant stem|stemmed]], [[rhizomatous]], or [[Begonia × tuberhybrida|tuberous]].  The plants are [[monoecious]], with unisexual male and female flowers occurring separately on the same plant; the male contains numerous [[stamen]]s, and the female has a large inferior [[ovary (plants)|ovary]] and two to four branched or twisted [[Carpel|stigma]]s. Flowers come in all colors other than blue, and range in size from tiny individual blossoms to blooms the size of dinner plates.<ref name="ABS">{{cite web |title=Basic Information |url=https://www.begonias.org/basic-information/ |website= begonias.org |publisher= [[American Begonia Society]] |access-date=19 December 2025 |ref=ABS}}</ref> In most species, the [[fruit]] is a winged [[capsule (fruit)|capsule]] containing numerous minute seeds, although [[baccate]] fruits are also known. The [[leaf|leaves]], which are often large and variously marked or [[variegation|variegated]], are usually asymmetric such that their left side and right side are different sizes. The plants vary in size from less than a foot to the recently discovered ''[[Begonia giganticaulis]]'' which can exceed twelve feet (3.6 meters) in height.

